\date{2026-07-28T17:20:38Z}
\import{macros}

\parent{fgap-000E}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{normalizer of a subgroup}

\tag{math}
\tag{fgap}
\tag{group}
\tag{normalizer}

\p{Let #{N} be a subgroup of #{G}. The \newvocab{normalizer} of #{N} in
#{G} is
##{
N_G(N)=\{g\in G:gNg^{-1}=N\}.
}
It is the largest subgroup of #{G} in which #{N} is normal. In particular, a
subgroup #{H\leq G} acts on #{N} by conjugation precisely when
##{
H\leq N_G(N).
}
See \citet{sec. 4.2.6, pp. 56--57}{fre2023discrete}.}

\p{The ambient group matters. If #{H} normalizes #{N}, then #{N} is normal
in the subgroup generated by #{N} and #{H}. This does not imply that #{N} is
normal in all of #{G}.}

\p{For the quaternion subgroups in [[fgap-0007]], the three conjugation
calculations in [[fgap-000C]] give
##{
C\leq N_{\mathbb{H}^{\times}}(Q).
}
They do not claim that #{Q} is normal in
#{\mathbb{H}^{\times}}.}
